  {
    "date": "2020-07-06",
    "term": "所",
    "kana": "ところ",
    "meaning": "place",
    "level": "N5",
    "pos": ["noun (common) (futsuumeishi)", "adverb (fukushi)", "suffix"],
    "priority": ["ichi1"],
    "stratum": "wago",
    "processes": ["compound", "clipping", "sound-change"],
    "headline": "Tokoro is likely cognate with 床 (toko, “raised area; bed”), and first attested in the Kojiki of 712 CE.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "⟨to₂ko₂ro₂⟩ → /tokoro/"
      },
      {
        "quote": "From Old Japanese. First attested in the Kojiki of 712 CE. From Proto-Japonic *təkərə. Likely cognate with 床 (toko, “raised area; bed”)."
      },
      {
        "quote": "A surface analysis suggests that this could be an ancient compound of ⟨to₂⟩ (Proto-Japonic *to (“place”), as in 跡 ⟨ato₂⟩, “footprint, track”, from “foot place, where one has stepped”) + ⟨ko₂⟩ (noun-forming suffixing element denoting “place”, as in ここ ⟨ko₂ko₂⟩, “here”, from “this place”) + ⟨ro₂⟩ (noun-forming suffixing element, apparently indicating within a general area, possibly as in 心 ⟨ko₂ko₂ro₂⟩, “heart, mind, emotions”, 頃 ⟨ko₂ro₂⟩, “general span of time, around a certain time”, うつろ ⟨uturo₂⟩, “hollowness, empty interior”)."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Listed in some sources as an abbreviation of tokoro above. However, this is also listed as cognate with 床 (toko, “raised area; bed”), which term already appears in use as far back as the Kojiki (712 CE), one of the oldest Japanese texts."
      }
    ],
    "examples": [
      {
        "id": 145729,
        "ja": "信じるとこよ。",
        "en": "I almost believe you.",
        "enId": 268833,
        "form": "とこ",
        "furigana": [["信", "しん"], ["じるとこよ。"]]
      },
